OpenBSD's malloc, used as Citadel's allocator.

Source: lib/libc/stdlib/malloc.c from OpenBSD
        ($OpenBSD: malloc.c,v 1.300 2025/10/23 18:49:46 miod Exp $)
        https://github.com/openbsd/src/blob/master/lib/libc/stdlib/malloc.c
License: ISC (see the header of malloc.c); copyright Otto Moerbeek,
        Matthew Dempsky, Damien Miller and Poul-Henning Kamp.

malloc.c is kept as close to upstream as possible, so updates can be
merged. Every local change is marked with a "CITADEL:" comment:

  - OpenBSD's headers are replaced by compat.h, which maps what the file
    expects (locks, the thread id, mimmutable, the options sysctl,
    sys/queue.h lists) onto Citadel's runtime;
  - no .openbsd.mutable section;
  - aligned_alloc accepts any size (C17, DR 460);
  - a free of a pointer into the middle of a chunk is rejected, not
    treated as a free of the whole chunk;
  - a region-growing mapping that lands away from its hint is unmapped;
  - malloc_usable_size is added at the end.

It is built with MALLOC_SMALL (no statistics or leak reports, which need
ktrace, a tree library and dladdr).

Defaults differ from OpenBSD's: Citadel turns on chunk canaries (C) and
guard pages after page-sized allocations (G), in the place of OpenBSD's
vm.malloc_conf sysctl. MALLOC_OPTIONS still overrides them, except in
setuid/setgid programs, where it is ignored. See malloc(3) on OpenBSD for
the option letters.

glue.c holds the fork and thread-start hooks and the functions
malloc.c does not define (reallocarray, memalign, valloc, pvalloc,
malloc_trim).
